{from UTF-8 to PDY}
INTRODUCTION

This volume contains material of mainly philological interest. It is for many parts in the first line a documentation of scanty knowledge and insufficient tools. Since the problems have been expounded at some length in the introduction to volume I, here merely a description of the working procedure and its results will be given.

(1)	Glossary
The glossary is based on a computer version of a KT word-index of the Rajam edition  (which has been kindly put at my disposal by Dr. Thomas Malten, Cologne), giving the items in alphabetical order without any kind of analysis. This version has been checked against the index of Krishnambal 1974 which contains some morphological analysis and gives meanings which are based on the glosses of (modern) commentaries. Though on the whole good and reliable, it remains, however, insufficient, and moreover the misprints are as numerous as usual for this kind of publication. The glossary as it stands follows Caminataiyar's edition and includes a number of more important variants.  It gives a morphological analysis and a meaning. Where possible the connection to the Dravidian etymological dictionary is traced, otherwise the TL is named as a source. Compounds, especially verbal compounds, are usually not included in the DED, as neither are many proper names of plants, animals and people. Apart from these cases, the DED for unclear reasons doesn't account for an after all considerable part of quite usual Akam vocabulary.  A special list of these items (2) is added at the end of the glossary. Similarly, only a small number of Indo-Aryan borrowings are noted in the DBIA. A list of the actual loanwords to be found in the KT (3) is added. The glossary also gives references to word-families/semantic fields (Â®) and to formulae ({Left-Right-double-Arrow}). When the semantic fields are too big, separate lists have been added (4); a number of frequent formulae are given in tables (5). The numerous complex verb forms, including veritable auxiliaries, also form a rubric of their own (6) , and the glossary refers to this (Â® auxiliary verbs). 

(2)	Tamil words not accounted for by the DED, DEDS, DEDR. 
This is simply a list of all the words (most of them apparently quite ordinary Tamil) which have, for some reason or the other, not been included in the Burrow/Emeneau dictionaries. Whether they have been forgotten, whether they are there but not included in the index or whether there are more unrecognised Indo-Aryan (or other?) words among them, it seemed useful to document the astonishing number and range of these items.

(3)	Indo-Aryan loanwords. (List of Tamil loanwords in Sanskrit.)
The supposed share of Indo-Aryan loanwords in Caïkam literature (low or high, mostly very low) has been a topic of ongoing controversy. The material basis for such a discussion, however, has never been provided: we simply don't know how many Sanskrit words there are in the different texts. This list of words to be found in the KT shows that actually there are quite many, though for a number of them the etymology is far from clear. The TL gives many more than probably can be justified (some of which are added in an additional list), while some similarities might rather be attributed to a Sanskrit borrowing of Dravidian items.

(4)	Semantic fields
Many parallel ways of expressing similar things are supposedly a problem in all highly developed poetic idioms, but the number of near synonyms or, more carefully, of items in a semantic field is at times simply baffling, and moreover, since for most of them we have no idea about shades of meaning or even syntactical differences, they present a severe obstacle when trying to translate Caïkam poems. This list is not supposed to be complete; it simply ought to give an impression of the issue by accumulating a number of relevant examples.

(5)	Formulae
A complete catalogue of the formulaic repertoire must be termed a desideratum of future Caïkam research. This list can only be called a beginning, providing the beginning and end formulae, as well as a number of formulae with structural relevance, to be found in the KT. 

(6)	Complex verbs
The problem of complex verb forms in Caïkam Tamil, be it noun-verb compounds or virtual auxiliary constructions, has not yet been realised to its full extent. Here the relevant material from the KT is collected and at times provided with tentative suggestions as to the function.

(7)	Finite Verbs / Participial Nouns
A complete list of the finite verb forms and the participial nouns to be found in the KT might be useful for further morphological and/or syntactical analysis.

The background consideration of the following three lists is the goal to provide anthology statistics as a material basis also of use to historians and anthropologists, here of course still restricted to the KT. They contain the designations connected with a region or an occupation including some of the tools used (like the musical instruments), the proper names of people and places, and the names of the authors with an analysis concerning type, meaning, place affiliation and Sanskrit elements.
(8)	Tribes / Castes / Professions
(9)	Proper Names
(10)	Authors

(11)	Inventory of Symbols
Here the term symbol has to be understood in the sense defined in the ambiguity section of chapter IV, namely as denoting an element in an inset supposedly provided with a second layer of meaning. This list too is probably far from complete, and it may contain regional requisites without symbolic value.





 
